<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puerto Rico</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puerto Rico is an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, colonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from the early sixteenth century. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, it was among the last remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> after the American independence wars of the early nineteenth century. Spain lost it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puerto-rico.jpg" alt="Puerto Rico" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: Cerejota · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Puerto_Rico.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Puerto Rico consists of a main island and smaller islands, including Vieques, Culebra and Mona. It lies about 1,000 miles south-east of Miami, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> and the U.S. Virgin Islands, and is the smallest of the Greater Antilles. The Cordillera Central crosses the island from east to west. Puerto Rico is Spanish for rich port. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> named the island San Juan Bautista and the capital Ciudad de Puerto Rico, but traders came to call the whole island Puerto Rico, and San Juan became the name of the port and capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The island's history began with the Ortoiroid culture, an Archaic hunter-gatherer population present as early as about 4,000 years ago. Around 500–200 BCE Saladoid peoples from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco River</a> basin migrated there, bringing sedentary agriculture and ceramics, and their ancestry became predominant in the populations that developed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>. The Taíno were the dominant Indigenous people when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> arrived in 1493. Their numbers fell sharply in the 16th century through introduced infectious diseases, exploitation by Spanish settlers and warfare.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> claimed the island after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s arrival and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> colonised it in 1508. It was a major military post in the wars between Spain and other European powers in the 16th, 17th and 18th centuries, and in 1593 Portuguese soldiers formed the first garrison of the San Felipe del Morro fortress. Spanish settlers, mainly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, and African slaves changed the cultural and demographic landscape. By the late 19th century a distinct Puerto Rican identity, combining European, African and Indigenous elements, had begun to emerge.</p><p>In the 19th century Puerto Rico and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s last two colonies in the New World. The Crown revived the Royal Decree of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charites">Graces</a> of 1815, offering free land to those who swore loyalty to the Crown and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, in the hope that independence movements would lose strength. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> acquired Puerto Rico in 1898 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>. Puerto Ricans have been U.S. citizens since 1917, and Congress approved a territorial constitution in 1952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Puerto Rico is officially the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, an unincorporated territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and its political status is a continuing subject of debate. San Juan is the capital. Spanish and English are the official languages, though Spanish predominates. Old San Juan has the Cathedral of San Juan Bautista, which holds the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Puerto Rico” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Spanish–American War, Spain ceded Cuba, <b>Puerto Rico</b>, Guam and the Philippines to the United States by the Treaty of Paris of 1898 for US$20 million, and Cuba, which had declared independence from the Spanish Empire that year, passed under an American military government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish American wars of independence (1808–1826) dissolved the mainland American empire quickly, leaving Spain with Cuba, <b>Puerto Rico</b> and the Philippines among its remaining major colonies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Juan Ponce de León, governor of <b>Puerto Rico</b>, sighted the east coast of the peninsula in April 1513, claimed it for Spain and named it La Florida, for the season of Pascua Florida.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Victory over Spain in 1898 brought <b>Puerto Rico</b>, Guam and the Philippines.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It has spread to the Dominican Republic, <b>Puerto Rico</b>, France and Canada, and major temples stand in Miami, Oakland, Boston, New York City and Washington, D.C.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish sought to convert them to Catholicism; in 1579 Caribs destroyed a Franciscan church at Aguada in <b>Puerto Rico</b> and killed five of its members.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
